<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antialcidas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 115–95 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antialcidas was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> king who ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a> in the late 2nd century BCE. He is known from his coins and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heliodorus-pillar">Heliodorus pillar</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, set up by his Greek ambassador Heliodorus, who called himself a devotee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasudeva">Vasudeva</a>. The inscription is among the earliest evidence of a Greek adopting a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> cult.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antialcidas.jpg" alt="Antialcidas" width="320" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antialcidas_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…on their coins, Zeus, Herakles, Athena and Apollo, but also took part in Indian religions: Heliodorus, ambassador of <b>Antialcidas</b>, called himself a devotee of Vāsudeva on his pillar at Vidisha around 113 BCE, the first known inscription of the Bhagavata movement, and Sirkap, founded by Demetrius, mixed Greek…</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
